menu "Linux Bridge Exporter (Power Base -> Linux OTBR host)"

    config LINUX_BRIDGE_EXPORTER_QUEUE_LEN
        int "Bounded TOPOLOGY export event queue depth"
        default 32
        help
            Depth of the FIFO queue between power_base_transport's
            on_topology_applied/on_node_offline bridge-observer callbacks
            (OT-task context, non-blocking enqueue only) and this
            component's own sending task. A full queue drops the newest
            TOPOLOGY event with a rate-limited warning rather than blocking
            the OT task or halting anything -- see
            components/linux_bridge_exporter's own header comment. The
            periodic full topology resync below recovers any dropped event.
            (Correction pass: SAMPLE events no longer share this queue at
            all -- see PENDING_SAMPLES_MAX in linux_bridge_exporter.c, a
            fixed-size, always-coalescing-by-uuid table sized to
            SENSOR_ROOT_AGGREGATOR_MAX_NODES, not a Kconfig value.)

    config LINUX_BRIDGE_EXPORTER_FULL_RESYNC_INTERVAL_MS
        int "Full topology + latest-sample resync interval (ms)"
        default 5000
        help
            Every this many ms, resend every topology_registry node's
            CURRENT state AND every sensor_root_aggregator node's CURRENT
            (most recent) sample to the Linux host, regardless of whether
            anything changed -- recovers from a dropped/coalesced-away
            event, a stale/rebuilt Linux registry, or a Linux OTBR host that
            just restarted, without waiting for that module's next sensor
            measurement (this follow-up's own explicit requirement).

    config LINUX_BRIDGE_EXPORTER_HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL_MS
        int "Bridge exporter heartbeat interval (ms)"
        default 10000
        help
            Liveness-only signal (power_base_uuid + uptime_ms + node_count);
            never authoritative -- the periodic full resync above is what
            actually reconciles Linux-side state.

    config LINUX_BRIDGE_EXPORTER_SAMPLE_MIN_INTERVAL_MS
        int "Minimum interval between two SAMPLE publishes for the SAME module_uuid (ms)"
        default 1000
        help
            Transport-load reduction (Power Base -> Linux, this pass): caps
            how often this component actually PUBLISHES a SAMPLE for any one
            module_uuid, regardless of how often that module's sensor
            measurement/aggregator storage updates -- neither the sensor
            measurement period nor the aggregator's own storage rate is
            changed by this setting, only how often the ALREADY-STORED
            latest value is mirrored to the Linux host.

            Precise unit (correction pass -- see linux_bridge_exporter.c's
            own top comment): this bounds LOGICAL SAMPLE PUBLISHES, not
            individual CoAP attempts. One logical publish is the whole
            bounded transmit_sample() attempt sequence for a uuid (1 initial
            attempt + up to 2 transient-failure retries, all re-reading the
            CURRENT sensor_root_aggregator value immediately before each
            individual attempt) -- that whole sequence counts as ONE publish
            against this interval, dispatched at most once per interval per
            uuid, never once per retry.

            ALL SAMPLE publish paths share this SAME per-uuid gate now,
            including the periodic full resync
            (LINUX_BRIDGE_EXPORTER_FULL_RESYNC_INTERVAL_MS): a resync tick
            only marks each uuid's current aggregator value as pending
            again, it never dispatches a SAMPLE directly -- so a reactive
            publish immediately followed by a resync tick can never produce
            a second SAMPLE publish for the same uuid inside one interval
            window. Worst-case SAMPLE convergence after a dropped/missed
            event is therefore bounded by resync_interval + at most one
            SAMPLE_MIN_INTERVAL_MS window, not exactly resync_interval.

            Rapid samples for the same uuid within this window keep
            coalescing (mark-pending, latest-wins) exactly as before; once
            the window reopens, the then-CURRENT aggregator value (never a
            cached/stale one) is published. If every attempt in a publish
            sequence hits a transient failure, the uuid is left pending
            again so the NEXT allowed window retries with whatever is
            current at that later time -- never a busy loop, never a
            replayed stale value.

            TOPOLOGY events (ATTACHED/UPDATED/DETACHED/OFFLINE, parent-state
            changes) are a COMPLETELY SEPARATE path (s_topology_pending_*/
            s_topology_queue) and are NOT subject to this limit at all --
            they are always sent immediately, including the periodic full
            TOPOLOGY resync.

endmenu
